task:
sqlparse.split loses track of nesting in anonymous BEGIN ... END blocks that hold procedural control flow. Each input below must split as stated. Line breaks in the inputs are as shown.

FOR ... DO ... END FOR gives one statement, which contains 'END FOR;':

BEGIN
    SELECT 1;
    FOR R DO
        SELECT 1;
    END FOR;
END;

An inline CASE inside IF gives one statement, which contains 'END AS COUNT;':

BEGIN
    SELECT 1;
    IF 1 THEN
        SELECT CASE WHEN 1 THEN 2 ELSE 3 END AS COUNT;
    ELSE
        SELECT 2;
    END IF;
END;

FOR UPDATE and FOR SHARE are not loops. This gives 2 statements, and the second contains 'SELECT 3;':

BEGIN
    SELECT * FROM foo FOR UPDATE;
    SELECT * FROM bar FOR SHARE;
END;
SELECT 3;

Sequential loops balance. This gives 2 statements, and the second contains 'SELECT 3;':

BEGIN
    FOR x IN select_query LOOP
        SELECT 1;
    END LOOP;
    FOR y IN select_query LOOP
        SELECT 2;
    END LOOP;
END;
SELECT 3;

A procedural CASE closed by END CASE balances. This gives 2 statements, and the second contains 'SELECT 3;':

BEGIN
    CASE val
        WHEN 1 THEN SELECT 'one';
        WHEN 2 THEN SELECT 'two';
        ELSE SELECT 'other';
    END CASE;
END;
SELECT 3;

Outside any block, 'SELECT * FROM foo FOR UPDATE; SELECT 3;' gives exactly ['SELECT * FROM foo FOR UPDATE;', 'SELECT 3;'].

Everything else must keep working, including the transaction forms of BEGIN, such as BEGIN; and BEGIN TRANSACTION;, DECLARE ... BEGIN blocks in CREATE statements, and CASE and IF inside procedures.
